## R5a proof: a single matte, camera-facing Polygon, alone under a distant
## light -- no shadow falls on it, so this fixture is safe to reuse once R7
## lands shadows. The same fixture also grounds the ray tracer's render
## against the z-buffer's own (tests/polygonzbuffer_test.cpp).
##
## A convex, regular pentagon -- five vertices, not four, the same shape and
## reasoning as tests/rib/polygon.rib's own (a 4-vertex fixture cannot tell
## a real vertex-count fix from a hardcoded-4 coincidence), scaled up from
## its unit radius so the silhouette clears both tests' pixel-count floors
## at this fixture's own Format.
Display "r5a_polygon.tif" "file" "rgba"
Format 320 240 1
Projection "perspective" "fov" [60]
# Explicit, not the RI_EPSILON-near default: a flat, camera-facing polygon
# is exactly the shape the near-clip precision defect corrupts hardest
# (AGENTS.md, "Flat or z-narrow geometry needs Clipping 0.5 50").
Clipping 0.5 50
Translate 0 0 5
WorldBegin
## This vertex order's Newell normal is (0, 0, 1), but matte's shader
## (shaders/gmanmatte.cpp) faceforwards it against the incident ray before
## shading, so the same "from"/"to" the disk fixture uses -- a strong -z
## component in the light's own travel direction -- reaches this face
## nearly head-on too (N.L ~ 0.96) instead of grazing it.
LightSource "ambientlight" 1 "intensity" [0.1]
LightSource "distantlight" 2 "intensity" [1.0] "lightcolor" [1 1 1]
	"from" [1 1 -5] "to" [0 0 0]
Sides 2
Surface "matte" "Ka" [0.3] "Kd" [0.8]
Polygon "P" [0 1.3 0  -1.236374 0.401722 0  -0.764121 -1.051722 0
             0.764121 -1.051722 0  1.236374 0.401722 0]
WorldEnd
